// Proofs for the identities barista.hana states, in the strategy language of
// docs/proving.md. An identity with no entry here is proved by `diagram`
// alone — this file holds exactly the claims that need direction.

// The contract claim, and the corpus's biggest goal: 351 boxes against 2
// once `inline` has opened it. It closes as a decision tree of three
// hypotheses, written as structured `cases` (docs/proving.md) and
// compiled to one checked derivation per side.
//
// Each split names its wire by address — as much of the box's name as the
// listing emphasised, read off the goal as the step before it leaves it.
//
// One state decides everything: `emit` dispatches on `equal(x.2,
// state::thirsty)`, which is `#uk`, and the driver has already identified
// that test with the precondition disjunction's own — one wire, two
// readers. In the false case `emit` answers a false flag and the
// postcondition holds vacuously, whatever the precondition said — the
// whole disjunction is moot. In the true case `emit` builds the PushBack
// event outright; the tuple rows (`tuple-cancel`, `as-tuple-built`,
// `equal-refl`) take the postcondition's shape guards apart until its
// symbol checks are the very boxes the precondition tested, and the two
// `is_symbol` splits — `#lq` on the payload's second field and `#oz` on
// its first — decide them, each false case discharged by `and-literal`,
// each true case folding the conjunction shut.
//
// The two `is_symbol` splits are written as *arms* and the state split is
// not, and the nesting is what the proof means rather than what it needs
// to name a wire: `#oz`'s readers are created inside `#lq`'s true case,
// so that is where the hypothesis deciding them is spent.
proof barista::customer_impl::emit_does_pre_and_post_is_constant =
    inline both(decide)
    cases(#uk) both(decide)
    cases(#lq) (
        true: both(decide) cases(#oz) (
            true: both(decide),
            false: both(decide)),
        false: both(decide))
    diagram;
